# [M6.4.3] D27 blinded corpus -- atomic-groups module.
#
# The --features refusal direction for otherwise-VALID PCRE2 syntax
# (contrast with syntax_errors.rxt, which is genuinely malformed syntax
# with the module ENABLED). This file is pcrec-CLI-level, not a PCRE2
# semantics claim -- MOD-0.3c's own module-gate mechanism.
#
# Two blocks per pattern:
#   1. no `features` line at all -- the module is DISABLED (pcrec's
#      default feature set is "std1" = {classes, modifiers}, and
#      atomic-groups is not in it) -- pcrec must refuse. Confirmed
#      directly against the refreshed, post-[M6.4.2] CLI: still refuses,
#      unchanged.
#   2. `features atomic-groups` -- the module is ENABLED. This corpus was
#      FIRST WRITTEN against a prebuilt, pre-implementation `build/pcrec`
#      (every atomic/possessive construct refused outright, "requires
#      module" or "enabled but not implemented yet" -- see
#      syntax_errors.rxt's own note on this), so this direction originally
#      asserted `perr` too. At the [M6.4.2] acceptance run (module now
#      real), that assertion was WRONG in exactly the way it deserved to
#      be: it was vacuously true before (the pre-module binary refused
#      EVERYTHING, gate open or shut, so `perr` here was never testing the
#      GATE at all, only the absence of an implementation) and is
#      genuinely false now that the module works. Fixed below to assert
#      the real match, confirmed both against libpcre2 and end-to-end
#      against the refreshed `build/pcrec` (`--emit-main`, compiled and
#      run) -- not just "does it compile".
#
# A finding worth an implementer's attention (recorded, not changed by
# this corpus): measured directly while the module was still
# unimplemented, `(?>...)` distinguished its two refusal reasons
# ("requires module" vs "is enabled but ... is not implemented yet"), but
# every possessive-quantifier spelling tested here gave the IDENTICAL
# "possessive quantifier requires module 'atomic-groups'" message in both
# directions at that time -- suggesting the two constructs' gating checks
# were not sharing one code path. Left as historical record; block 2
# below no longer exercises that message at all now that the module is
# implemented and block 2 compiles cleanly.

# --- (?>...) ---

pattern (?>ab)
perr

pattern (?>ab)
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 2

# --- a++ ---

pattern a++
perr

pattern a++
features atomic-groups
m "aaab" 0 3

# --- a*+ ---

pattern a*+
perr

pattern a*+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3

# --- a?+ ---

pattern a?+
perr

pattern a?+
features atomic-groups
m "a" 0 1

# --- a{2,3}+ (bounded brace-possessive) ---

pattern a{2,3}+
perr

pattern a{2,3}+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3

# --- a{,3}+ (PCRE2 10.43+ {,n} spelling, possessive) ---

pattern a{,3}+
perr

pattern a{,3}+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3

# --- a{2,}+ (unbounded brace-possessive) ---

pattern a{2,}+
perr

pattern a{2,}+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3
